  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "flamenco-tunes-forms-history-and-recordings",
      "title": "Flamenco — Tunes — Forms, History, and Recordings",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Iberian Folk | **Region:** iberian-flamenco | **Tier:** 4.5\n**Prerequisites:** regions-iberian-flamenco-07-representative-depth\n**Tags:** iberian, flamenco, iberian, tunes, —"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Soleá, Bulerías listening\n\nFlamenco belongs to the **Iberian Folk** curriculum. Paco de Lucía\nCamarón\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **Soleá, Bulerías listening** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-galicia",
      "title": "Compared with Galicia",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Galician Traditional Music** (`regions-iberian-galicia-08-tunes`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **Soleá, Bulerías listening**.\n\n| Topic | Galicia anchor | Flamenco (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | Muiñeira de Chantada… | Soleá, Bulerías listening… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-iberian-galicia-08-tunes`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-08-tunes`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Soleá, Bulerías listening\n\nStudy **flamenco** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Flamenco](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flamenco) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tune-studies",
      "title": "Tune studies",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Representative repertoire includes **Soleá, Bulerías listening**. Use the lesson tune panel and **Play all** to compare multiple settings of the same title or form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Iberian Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **cante, guitar**. Ensemble layer: **palmas, percussion**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **palos, compás**. Dance context: **zapateado, braceo**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **cafés cantantes, revival**. Contemporary scenes: **tablaos, festivals**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Soleá, Bulerías listening**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Soleá, Bulerías listening\n- Lead instruments: cante, guitar\n- Ensemble: palmas, percussion\n- Forms: palos, compás\n- Dance: zapateado, braceo\n- History: cafés cantantes, revival\n- Scenes: tablaos, festivals\n- Repertoire: Soleá, Bulerías listening\n- Compared with Galicia: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Flamenco is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: Soleá, Bulerías listening.\n- Compared with Galicia: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: palos, compás.\n- Dance: zapateado, braceo.\n- Transmission: cafés cantantes, revival."
        }
      ]
    },
